biographies](/themes/custom/trustme/images/header-logo.jpg)\n\n# Auchincloss, 
Louis (Stanton)\n\n# AUCHINCLOSS, Louis (Stanton)\n\n**Nationality:** American. 
**Born:** Lawrence, [New 
York](/places/united-states-and-canada/us-political-geography/new-york), 27 
September\n1917. **Education:** Groton School, Massachusetts, graduated 1935; 
[Yale 
University](/social-sciences-and-law/education/colleges-us/yale-university), [New
Haven](/places/united-states-and-canada/us-political-geography/new-haven), 
Connecticut, 1935-38; [University of 
Virginia](/social-sciences-and-law/education/colleges-us/university-virginia) Law
School, Charlottesville, LL.B. 1941; admitted to the [New 
York](/places/united-states-and-canada/us-political-geography/new-york) bar, 
1941. **Military Service:** Served in the [United 
States](/places/united-states-and-canada/us-political-geography/united-states) 
Naval Reserve, 1941-45: Lieutenant. **Family:** Married Adele Lawrence in 1957; 
three sons. **Career:** Associate lawyer, Sullivan and Cromwell, [New 
York](/places/united-states-and-canada/us-political-geography/new-york), 1941-51;
associate, 1954-58, and partner, 1958-86, Hawkins Delafield and Wood, New York. 
Since 1966 president of the Museum of the City of New York. Trustee, Josiah Macy 
Jr. Foundation, New York; former member of the Executive Committee, Association 
of [the Bar](/social-sciences-and-law/law/law/bar) of New York City. **Awards:** 
New York State Governor's award, 1985. D. Litt.: New York University, 1974; Pace 
College, New York, 1979; University of [the 
South](/places/united-states-and-canada/miscellaneous-us-geography/south), 
Sewanee, 
[Tennessee](/places/united-states-and-canada/us-political-geography/tennessee), 
1986. **Member:** American Academy. **Agent:** Curtis Brown, 10 Astor Place, New 
York, New York 10003. **Address:** 1111 Park Avenue, New York, New York 10128, 
U.S.A.\n\n### Publications\n\n#### Novels\n\n*The Indifferent Children* (as 
Andrew Lee). New York, Prentice Hall, 1947.\n\n*Sybil.* Boston, Houghton Mifflin,
1951; London, Gollancz, 1952.\n\n*A Law for the Lion.* Boston, Houghton Mifflin, 
and London, Gollancz, 1953.\n\n*The Great World and Timothy Colt.* Boston, 
Houghton Mifflin, 1956;London, Gollancz, 1957.\n\n*Venus in 
[Sparta](/history/ancient-greece-and-rome/ancient-history-greece/sparta).* 
Boston, Houghton Mifflin, and London, Gollancz, 1958.\n\n*Pursuit of the 
Prodigal.* Boston, Houghton Mifflin, 1959; London, Gollancz, 1960.\n\n*The House 
of Five Talents.* Boston, Houghton Mifflin, 1960; London, Gollancz, 
1961.\n\n*Portrait in Brownstone.* Boston, Houghton Mifflin, and London, 
Gollancz, 1962.\n\n*The Rector of Justin.* Boston, Houghton Mifflin, 1964; 
London, Gollancz, 1965.\n\n*The Embezzler.* Boston, Houghton Mifflin, and London,
Gollancz1966.\n\n*A World of Profit.* Boston, Houghton Mifflin, 1968; London, 
Gollancz, 1969.\n\n*I Come as a Thief.* Boston, Houghton Mifflin, 1972; London, 
Weidenfeld and Nicolson, 1973.\n\n*The Partners.* Boston, Houghton Mifflin, and 
London, Weidenfeld and Nicolson, 1974.\n\n*The Winthrop Covenant.* Boston, 
Houghton Mifflin, and London, Weidenfeld and Nicolson, 1976.\n\n*The Dark Lady.* 
Boston, Houghton Mifflin, and London, Weidenfeld and Nicolson, 1977.\n\n*The 
Country Cousin.* Boston, Houghton Mifflin, and London, Weidenfeld and Nicolson, 
1978.\n\n*The House of the Prophet.* Boston, Houghton Mifflin, and London, 
Weidenfeld and Nicolson, 1980.\n\n*The Cat and the King.* Boston, Houghton 
Mifflin, and London, Weidenfeld and Nicolson, 1981.\n\n*Watchfires.* Boston, 
Houghton Mifflin, and London, Weidenfeld andNicolson, 1982.\n\n*Exit Lady 
Masham.* Boston, Houghton Mifflin, 1983; London, Weidenfeld and Nicolson, 
1984.\n\n*The Book Class.* Boston, Houghton Mifflin, and London, Weidenfeld and 
Nicolson, 1984.\n\n*Honorable Men…(truncated)"}, {"url": 
"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/The_Rector_of_Justin", "title": "The Rector of 
Justin - Wikipedia", "content": "# *The Rector of Justin*. ***The Rector of 
Justin*** is a 1964 psychological fiction novel by Louis Auchincloss about the 
headmaster (or \"rector \"Rector (ecclesiastical)\")\") of a socially exclusive 
American boarding school. Through the narrators' disagreements, the novel 
gradually unveils that White Anglo-Saxon Protestant society—of which Prescott is 
a reluctant mascot—has lost its innocence and abandoned its Christian values. The
