    "raw_content": "The Rector of Justin by Louis Auchincloss\n\nMarch 27, 2023 
by vel veeter\n\nThis is a decidedly tender novel that I wasn’t exactly expecting
to be as good as it is. For one thing, I just read some Louis Auchincloss 
nonfiction, and while his thinking is very strong in those essays, the writing 
itself felt too pedestrian or pragmatic. But here, the language is very strong, 
lively, and breathed with spirit. Perhaps it’s the change in form or just the 25 
years in between.\n\nThe novel is also unexpected in its form. We meet a young 
teacher on the first week of his career. He’s joined the staff of a private 
Christian school, the Martyr of Justin school, a stand-in for Groton, which 
Auchincloss worked for many years prior. And since I’ve read a lot of novels in 
which an author is looking back at his earlier days as a teacher, I thought I 
knew where we were headed. But this is NOT Goodbye Mr Chips, and it’s also not as
weird as The Magus. In fact, maybe I should retract my sense that there’s a 
typical teacher book.\n\nAnyway, the novel takes this young teacher who’s been 
hired with no experience and maybe no real ambition. But when he arrives, and 
starts feeling immediately lost, he’s eventually taken under the wing of the 
rector, the headmaster and head minister, Dr Presscott. The book then is less 
about his experience of a young teacher and more about trying to learn everything
he can about this man and what comprises him and his character.\n\nThe novel is 
primarily told through journals and diaries, but then takes on long sections of 
other texts that help to answer the question of who this man is — an unpublished 
memoir of a childhood friend, an interview with his daughter, the journals of 
another young teacher of a previous generation.\n\n[Further paragraphs focus more
on thematic commentary, stylistic comparisons, and the reviewer’s reaction than 
on additional plot detail.]",
    "summary": "Community review that sketches the novel’s premise and structure:
a young, inexperienced teacher at the Justin Martyr school is mentored by rector 
Dr. Prescott and becomes intent on understanding his character. Describes the 
narrative technique of using journals, diaries, a childhood friend’s memoir, an 
interview with Prescott’s daughter, and another teacher’s journals as extended 
embedded texts. Confirms the boarding-school setting, the rector’s dual role as 
headmaster and minister, and the investigative nature of the narrator’s project. 
Less detailed on specific events or the ending than the Pugh analysis, but 
consistent with it on framing and key characters."
