Ivy.” The review discusses the novel as a serious, literate exploration of the 
character and moral compromises of an aging headmaster of an elite boys’ boarding
school. Granville Hicks treats the school as a microcosm for the American 
upper-class establishment and notes Auchincloss’s skill in portraying the 
rector’s charisma, authority, and inner conflicts. The review frames the book as 
one of Auchincloss’s most ambitious works, emphasizes its psychological 
portraiture, and comments on the effectiveness of its multiple-narrator structure
and its critique of privilege in the context of mid‑20th‑century American 
society. It contains several evaluative judgments about the excellence of 
Auchincloss’s characterization and the novel’s insight into institutional 
leadership and moral ambiguity.",
