Auchincloss](/wiki/Louis_Auchincloss \"Louis Auchincloss\") about the headmaster 
(or \"[rector](/wiki/Rector_(ecclesiastical) \"Rector (ecclesiastical)\")\") of a
socially exclusive American [boarding school](/wiki/Boarding_school \"Boarding 
school\"). Over the decades, various narrators provide contrasting perspectives 
on rector Francis Prescott's charismatic personality and autocratic leadership 
style. Through the narrators' disagreements, the novel gradually unveils that 
[White Anglo-Saxon Protestant](/wiki/White_Anglo-Saxon_Protestants \"White 
Anglo-Saxon Protestants\") society—of which Prescott is a reluctant mascot—has 
lost its innocence and abandoned its Christian values.\n\nThe novel was a 
commercial hit and was warmly received by most contemporary critics. It was a 
finalist for the [Pulitzer Prize for Fiction](/wiki/Pulitzer_Prize_for_Fiction 
\"Pulitzer Prize for Fiction\") and the [National Book Award for 
Fiction](/wiki/National_Book_Award_for_Fiction \"National Book Award for 
Fiction\"), losing to [Shirley Ann Grau](/wiki/Shirley_Ann_Grau \"Shirley Ann 
Grau\")'s *[The Keeper of the House](/wiki/The_Keepers_of_the_House \"The Keepers
of the House\")* and [Saul Bellow](/wiki/Saul_Bellow \"Saul Bellow\")'s 
*[Herzog](/wiki/Herzog_(novel) \"Herzog (novel)\")*, respectively. Its loss to 
*Herzog* marked [American literary 
fiction](/wiki/American_literature#Post-World_War_II_fiction \"American 
literature\")'s transition from realist [society novels](/wiki/Society_novel 
