the novel centers on Frank Prescott, founder of an exclusive New England boys' 
school, and is regarded as one of Auchincloss's most accomplished works. It 
explains that Prescott's character, both admirable and enigmatic, is gradually 
revealed through the perspectives of people drawn into his charismatic orbit. 
These include Brian Aspinwall, a young teacher and Prescott's latest protégé; 
Horace Havistock, his oldest friend who is now a conspicuous failure; Prescott's 
daughter, who concludes that her father has always loved the school more than his
family; and a wealthy trustee who believes that Prescott is as much a product of 
the school as its creator. The copy emphasizes the novel's wit and elegance and 
notes that it is widely recognized as Auchincloss's masterpiece. The description 
