      "source_ref": "s1",
      "supports_or_contradicts": "c2",
      "confidence": "high"
    },
    {
      "local_ref": "e3",
      "statement": "The analysis notes that Prescott \"ran the school for almost 
60 years very much as his old headmaster had, becoming more and more out of touch
with the increasingly secular orientation of the school’s board of trustees and 
with growing disenchantment with his harshly religiously doctrinaire and 
authoritarian style.\"",
      "document_ref": "d1",
      "source_ref": "s1",
      "supports_or_contradicts": "c3",
      "confidence": "high"
    },
    {
      "local_ref": "e4",
      "statement": "According to Pugh, Prescott \"was finally talked into 
retiring at age 82 by the school’s board’s influential head trustee (David 
Griscam)\" and after moving near campus \"tried to undermine the new headmaster 
for a while but finally realized that opinion has turned against him. He became 
terminally ill four years later, was initially at peace with his prognosis, but 
later torn by fears of judgment in the next world.\"",
      "document_ref": "d1",
      "source_ref": "s1",
      "supports_or_contradicts": "c4",
      "confidence": "high"
    },
    {
      "local_ref": "e5",
      "statement": "The blog states that Prescott \"had three daughters whom he 
admitted not to loving enough, and deeply regretted that he didn’t have a son; 
the first two daughters faded into family life, while the third was a persistent 
thorn in his side.\"",
      "document_ref": "d1",
      "source_ref": "s1",
      "supports_or_contradicts": "c5",
      "confidence": "high"
    },
    {
      "local_ref": "e6",
      "statement": "In describing key relationships, the analysis mentions 
Prescott’s dealings \"with whom and with whose soul-sick son, Jules, he had had a
long and troubled relationship,\" referring to David Griscam and Jules Griscam.",
      "document_ref": "d1",
      "source_ref": "s1",
      "supports_or_contradicts": "c6",
      "confidence": "high"
    },
    {
      "local_ref": "e7",
      "statement": "Pugh identifies Brian Aspinwall as \"a new English literature
instructor (Brian Aspinwall), who arrives about two years before Prescott’s 
retirement\" and notes that the tale \"is entrusted (by Prescott and an 
interesting variety of other parties with varying motivations)\" to Brian.",
      "document_ref": "d1",
      "source_ref": "s1",
      "supports_or_contradicts": "c7",
      "confidence": "high"
    },
    {
      "local_ref": "e8",
      "statement": "The same post explains that \"These accounts, in addition to 
several entries from Aspinwall’s diary, make up this unusual novel, which unfolds
entirely in the words of Brian or his informants and largely in their own 
documentary words. One narrator, Horace Havistock, was Prescott’s only childhood 
friend. Another is his youngest daughter. The others are a wealthy man, David 
Griscam... and his soul-sick son, Jules.\"",
      "document_ref": "d1",
      "source_ref": "s1",
      "supports_or_contradicts": "c8",
      "confidence": "high"
    },
    {
      "local_ref": "e9",
      "statement": "The analysis argues that \"this is a mystery story, with an 
amateur detective who remains puzzled by the extraordinary success of an 
Episcopal boys’ school\" and that \"many of the questions they raise about both 
Prescott and his school remain unresolved at the end of the novel,\" leaving an 
impression of \"a missing ingredient that we are supposed to figure out for 
ourselves.\"",
      "document_ref": "d1",
      "source_ref": "s1",
      "supports_or_contradicts": "c9",
      "confidence": "medium"
    },
    {
      "local_ref": "e10",
      "statement": "Pugh describes the book as having \"not a lot of action\" but
functioning as \"the iterative mystery of Prescott himself\" and concludes that 
\"This really is a character study dressed up like a detective story, presented 
in a chronological sequence, and involving an ensemble cast of supporting 
characters.\"",
      "document_ref": "d1",
      "source_ref": "s1",
      "supports_or_contradicts": "c10",
      "confidence": "high"
    },
    {
      "local_ref": "e11",
      "statement": "The Cannonball Read review states that \"We meet a young 
teacher on the first week of his career. He’s joined the staff of a private 
Christian school, the Martyr of Justin school, a stand-in for Groton, which 
Auchincloss worked for many years prior.\"",
      "document_ref": "d2",
      "source_ref": "s2",
      "supports_or_contradicts": "c11",
      "confidence": "high"
    },
    {
      "local_ref": "e12",
      "statement": "The reviewer notes that when the young teacher arrives and 
feels lost, \"he’s eventually taken under the wing of the rector, the headmaster 
and head minister, Dr Presscott\" and that the book is \"less about his 
experience of a young teacher and more about trying to learn everything he can 
about this man and what comprises him and his character.\"",
      "document_ref": "d2",
      "source_ref": "s2",
      "supports_or_contradicts": "c12",
      "confidence": "high"
    },
    {
      "local_ref": "e13",
      "statement": "The review explains that \"The novel is primarily told 
through journals and diaries, but then takes on long sections of other texts that
help to answer the question of who this man is — an unpublished memoir of a 
childhood friend, an interview with his daughter, the journals of another young 
teacher of a previous generation.\"",
      "document_ref": "d2",
      "source_ref": "s2",
      "supports_or_contradicts": "c13",
      "confidence": "high"
    },
    {
      "local_ref": "e14",
      "statement": "Kirkus opens by stating: \"The Rector is the Reverend Francis
Prescott, D.D., the founder-headmaster of Justin Martyr, a venerable, Episcopal 
New England secondary school of high caste and character.\"",
      "document_ref": "d3",
      "source_ref": "s3",
      "supports_or_contradicts": "c14",
      "confidence": "high"
    },
    {
      "local_ref": "e15",
      "statement": "The Kirkus review calls the book \"a prismatic portrait of 
Dr. Prescott, who has now reached the end of some eighty forceful years, as seen 
through the sometimes coincident, sometimes contradictory interpretations of his 
family and associates\" and notes that \"Brian Aspinwall, a shy epicene young 
protege of Prescott's assembles the evidence.\"",
      "document_ref": "d3",
      "source_ref": "s3",
      "supports_or_contradicts": "c15",
      "confidence": "high"
    },
    {
      "local_ref": "e16",
      "statement": "Kirkus lists the sources Brian uses: \"the memoir of a 
decadent dilettante, once Prescott's school friend and satellite; the verbal 
confidences of his third daughter-- another Cordelia, whose love affair he had 
demolished; the testimony of one of his first students who became one of the 
school's trustees, and of that man's son whom he had expelled in a particularly 
vindictive episode; etc. etc.\"",
      "document_ref": "d3",
      "source_ref": "s3",
      "supports_or_contradicts": "c16",
      "confidence": "high"
    },
    {
      "local_ref": "e17",
      "statement": "The same review asks rhetorically, \"Leviathan? legend? 
charlatan? man of God or God among men? Prescott was many things to many people\"
to underscore the varied and conflicting perceptions of his character.",
      "document_ref": "d3",
      "source_ref": "s3",
      "supports_or_contradicts": "c17",
      "confidence": "high"
    },
    {
      "local_ref": "e18",
      "statement": "Kirkus remarks that this is \"Auchincloss' first novel in 
some years which has gone beyond and below the superficial sheen of New York and 
Newport to provide a story of more substance\" and notes that \"Its selection by 
the Book-of-the-Month club should extend a sphere of influence outside the 
ivy-and-moss covered purlieu of New England academia.\"",
      "document_ref": "d3",
      "source_ref": "s3",
      "supports_or_contradicts": "c18",
      "confidence": "high"
    },
    {
      "local_ref": "e19",
      "statement": "The Amazon publisher description states that the novel 
\"centers on Frank Prescott, the founder of an exclusive school for boys in New 
England\" and that \"As his story is told through the eyes of those drawn into 
his charismatic orbit, Prescott's character—at once admirable and enigmatic—is 
gradually revealed.\"",
      "document_ref": "d4",
      "source_ref": "s4",
      "supports_or_contradicts": "c19",
      "confidence": "high"
    },
    {
      "local_ref": "e20",
      "statement": "The same description lists the key observers: \"There is 
Brian Aspinwall, a young teacher and the rector's latest protégé; Horace 
Havistock, Prescott's oldest friend, who is now a conspicuous failure; Francis's 
daughter, who concludes that her father has always loved the school more than his
family; and finally, a wealthy trustee who understands that Prescott is as much 
the product of the school as its creator.\"",
      "document_ref": "d4",
      "source_ref": "s4",
      "supports_or_contradicts": "c20",
      "confidence": "high"
    },
    {
      "local_ref": "e21",
      "statement": "The publisher copy asserts that \"THE RECTOR OF JUSTIN is 
told with wit and elegance; it is a wise and resonant work, widely recognized as 
Auchincloss's masterpiece.\"",
      "document_ref": "d4",
      "source_ref": "s4",
      "supports_or_contradicts": "c21",
      "confidence": "medium"
    },
    {
      "local_ref": "e22",
      "statement": "Pugh notes that the novel \"unfolds entirely in the words of 
Brian or his informants and largely in their own documentary words,\" while 
Kirkus similarly describes Brian as assembling memoirs, confidences, and 
testimony, and the Cannonball Read review emphasizes journals, diaries, an 
unpublished childhood friend's memoir, an interview with Prescott's daughter, and
another teacher's journals as narrative vehicles.",
      "document_ref": "d1",
      "source_ref": "s1",
      "supports_or_contradicts": "c22",
      "confidence": "high"
    },
    {
      "local_ref": "e23",
      "statement": "Kirkus refers to \"Brian Aspinwall, a shy epicene young 
protege of Prescott's\" who assembles evidence; Amazon calls Brian \"a young 
teacher and the rector's latest protégé\"; Pugh depicts Brian as a new English 
literature instructor entrusted with accounts; the Cannonball Read review 
describes a young teacher taken under Dr. Prescott's wing and focused on learning
about him.",
      "document_ref": "d3",
      "source_ref": "s3",
      "supports_or_contradicts": "c23",
      "confidence": "high"
    }
  ],
  "conflicts": []
}
