\n\n# A Compendium of Novel Structure 
Resources\n\n![](https://stormwritingschool.com/wp-content/uploads/2017/06/12-1.p
ng)\n\nIn preparation for a class I’m teaching this weekend, I’ve done an 
exhaustive exploration of novel and story structure advice. Below, I’ve assembled
what is more or less a bibliography. Consider this a list of resources more than 
the typical craft tip article. Bookmark this one and come back to it for future 
reads. (For my next article, I hope to compile some thoughts about the common 
elements among all of these structure paradigms.)\n\nI have first and foremost 
**Eva Deverell** to thank for this. She has a superb [article on plot 
formulas](http://www.eadeverell.com/plot-formulas/) on her site, wherein she 
defines plot formulas as “any simplified story framework that helps you create 
your own story.” I’ve always been a bit wary of plot formulas, but I can get 
behind Deverell’s definition. In her article, she discusses over 15 plot 
paradigms, and she has a downloadable “[plot formula cheat 
sheet](http://www.eadeverell.com/wp-content/uploads/EA-Deverell-Plot-Formula-Chea
tsheet.pdf),” which summarizes several of them. Her article and her cheatsheet 
were launching pads for my own research. (And in fact, I assembled a [cheat 
sheet](https://drive.google.com/file/d/0Byo2roXnp438WTNjSVpVWHNobHc/view?usp=shar
ing) which includes some of Deverell’s summaries and adds several more.)\n\nNow, 
my objective here is not to promote formulas at all but just to provide a survey 
of what’s out there. It’s an intimidating world, but a novel is an ambitious 
undertaking, and even if you’re going to approach it by discovering the story as 
you write, it’s still worth getting familiar with story structure because 
eventually, you’re going to need it. After looking into all of these structures, 
I’ve come away with a pretty good idea of the imperatives. But that’s an article 
for a different day.\n\n### Acts\n\nBefore I delve into the structures, a word 
about acts. **C.S. Lakin** has a great [article about the arbitrary nature of 
acts](http://www.livewritethrive.com/2016/05/02/what-writers-may-not-know-about-t
he-required-three-act-structure/). Act divisions are often for purposes other 
than story optimization. Sometimes, they just serve to give the audience a chance
to use the bathroom; sometimes, they’re for commercial breaks (if you’re writing 
for TV like Lakin’s mother did). I would add that you can retroactively fit 
almost any act structure on any story. James N. Frey, in his book *[How to Write 
a Damn Good 
Thriller](https://www.goodreads.com/book/show/7006648-how-to-write-a-damn-good-th
riller?ac=1&from_search=true)*, promotes a five-act structure. But the first two 
movies he uses to exemplify his structure are Fargo and Alien, which I rewatched,
and both of them can very easily be broken into 3 or 4 acts.\n\nIn short, I don’t
know how useful acts really are, but as we all know, stories have a beginning, a 
middle, and an ending, and the three-act paradigm arises out of that very simple 
and logical fact. There’s no doubt that the three-act framework is the most 
popular among structure gurus. You can, however, overlay a five-act framework on 
top of it, as **Shawn Coyne** does in his book The Story Grid. Coyne talks about 
a three-act structure, but also “[five 
commandments](http://www.storygrid.com/466/),” which include inciting incident, 
progressive complications, crisis, climax, and resolution. One could argue that 
every paradigm includes both three acts ([beginning hook, middle build, ending 
payoff](http://www.storygrid.com/hook-build-payoff/), as Coyne calls them) and 
the five commandments (which align more or less with Frey’s five-act structure). 
The inciting incident occurs in the beginning hook; the middle build is all about
