"https://janefriedman.com/story-structure", "title": "Classic Story Structures 
and What They Teach Us About Novel Plotting | Jane Friedman", "content": "The 
first act includes the story setup, popularly referred to as the “inciting 
incident.” The stakes continue to rise in the second act and include a false 
victory, that point where you think the story is over but it turns out it’s not. 
Coincidentally, my ancestors on my mom’s side are from Germany.) Freytag’s main 
changes were to allow for more setup of the main character’s motivation in the 
beginning and to put the climax at the dead middle, which gives as much time to 
the untying at the end as to the setting up at the beginning. We learn about 
Dorothy’s dissatisfaction at home, much as we learn of Luke’s in the beginning of
Star Wars, but Dorothy has the added motivation of needing to save Toto from 
being euthanized, escaping an incoming tornado, and squishing the Wicked Witch of
the East, all of which weight the front end of The Wizard of Oz more heavily than
was Star Wars’ first act.", "score": 0.14382415, "raw_content": "![Jane 
