  "documents": [
    {
      "local_ref": "d1",
      "document_summary": "Ira Stoll’s 2020 Education Next article discusses 
Louis Auchincloss’s 1964 novel The Rector of Justin in the context of teaching, 
education reform, and contemporary politics. It quotes a New York Times obituary 
describing The Rector of Justin as a bestseller and a finalist for the National 
Book Award and as regarded by many critics as Auchincloss’s best and most 
important novel, summarizing its protagonist Frank Prescott as a prewar New 
England boys’ school headmaster who embodies both idealism and moral ambivalence.
The article quotes several passages from the novel about teaching, the unity of 
pulpit and classroom, religious presence in school life, and the value of private
schools in improving mediocre students. Stoll comments that the book still holds 
up decades after its publication and uses it to reflect on enduring debates over 
private schooling, religion, and educational ideals. The piece thus provides 
secondary commentary on the novel’s themes, later reception, and perceived 
significance, relying in part on the NYT obituary and on direct quotations from 
the text.",
      "source_ref": "s1"
    },
    {
      "local_ref": "d2",
      "document_summary": "The American Legends piece titled \"The Rector of 
Justin\" presents a narrative essay about the culture and history of elite New 
England boarding schools and their headmasters. It evokes a \"golden era\" of 
once-aristocratic New England boarding schools and describes their headmasters as
aristocratic or near-aristocratic figures who shaped generations of leaders. The 
article highlights several notable headmasters, including Frank Boyden of 
Deerfield and George Van Santvoord of Hotchkiss, but gives particular emphasis to
Endicott Peabody of Groton. It portrays Peabody as fair, athletic, pious, and a 
member of a prominent Massachusetts family, notes that students addressed him as 
\"the Rector,\" and recounts anecdotes showing his strict and intimidating 
demeanor, citing Geoffrey Ward’s biography of Franklin Roosevelt for one such 
story. The text provides contextual material about Groton School, the role and 
image of its rector, and the ethos of elite prep school leadership, which can be 
used to frame discussions of real-world archetypes relevant to a fictional rector
in The Rector of Justin.",
      "source_ref": "s2"
    },
    {
      "local_ref": "d3",
      "document_summary": "The National Book Foundation’s page for The Rector of 
Justin lists the novel in its awards database as a finalist for the 1965 National
Book Awards in the Fiction category. The entry provides the book’s title, the 
author’s name, and the award year and clearly labels its status as \"Finalist, 
National Book Awards 1965 for Fiction.\" The page may also include basic 
bibliographic or promotional elements and navigation related to award years and 
categories, but its key function is to document the novel’s official standing in 
the National Book Awards history. This source serves as an authoritative record 
for the book’s award status, distinguishing finalist recognition from winning the
award.",
      "source_ref": "s3"
    },
    {
      "local_ref": "d4",
      "document_summary": "The Fall 2010 issue of Groton School Quarterly 
includes an In Memoriam obituary for Louis S. Auchincloss, class of 1935, 
