submit_final_report({'title': 'The Rector of Justin', 'sections': [{'title': 
'Lead', 'blocks': [{'text': "The Rector of Justin is a 1964 novel by American 
writer Louis Auchincloss, first published in Boston by Houghton Mifflin.[1][2][3]
Centered on Frank (Francis) Prescott, the longtime rector and headmaster of 
Justin Martyr, an exclusive Episcopal boys' secondary school in New England, the 
book is structured as a composite portrait assembled from multiple narrators and 
documentary sources.[12][14][21] Often described as one of Auchincloss's most 
accomplished works and widely recognized as his masterpiece, it was a bestseller 
and a finalist for the 1965 National Book Award for Fiction.[16][21][27]", 
'source_ids': ['20260623024132065431_f121819f', '20260623024132078317_adc75180', 
'20260623024132089245_53004c14', '20260623024307745077_3db5b612', 
'20260623024307767432_483bea11', '20260623024310821544_600a050b', 
'20260623024307789750_523a064b', '20260623024643574785_d622c032'], 'kind': 
'paragraph'}]}, {'title': 'Background and publication', 'blocks': [{'text': 'The 
Rector of Justin was published in 1964 by Houghton Mifflin in Boston, with the 
first U.S. hardcover edition running to approximately 341 pages.[1][2][3] A U.K. 
edition followed from Gollancz in London in 1965, indicating early transatlantic 
interest in the novel.[7] Later reprintings include a 1980 Houghton Mifflin 
edition recorded in WorldCat and a widely available Mariner Books paperback 
edition with ISBN 0618224890, published in the early 2000s.[29][31]', 
'source_ids': ['20260623024132065431_f121819f', '20260623024132078317_adc75180', 
'20260623024132089245_53004c14', '20260623024132132466_776229e4', 
'20260623024643651710_5aaa1047', '20260623024643677485_51f1bfc6'], 'kind': 
'paragraph'}, {'text': 'Library and bookseller records consistently attribute the
novel to Louis Auchincloss and list Houghton Mifflin as the original publisher; 
several 1964 copies are described in the antiquarian book trade as first editions
or first printings, suggesting that Houghton Mifflin’s initial run has collector 
significance.[2][4][5][6]', 'source_ids': ['20260623024132078317_adc75180', 
'20260623024132099428_a42b4739', '20260623024132110240_515cff7d', 
'20260623024132121106_8cfcc34b'], 'kind': 'paragraph'}, {'text': 'Auchincloss was
an alumnus of Groton School, a prominent New England boarding school, graduating 
in the class of 1935 before pursuing a dual career as a lawyer and novelist; 
institutional obituaries from Groton identify The Rector of Justin among his 
notable works, linking his education in an elite boarding environment to his 
later fiction about such institutions.[28]', 'source_ids': 
['20260623024643631866_3f1971d6'], 'kind': 'paragraph'}]}, {'title': 'Setting and
relationship to real schools', 'blocks': [{'text': 'Within the novel, Justin 
Martyr is portrayed as a venerable, high‑status Episcopal New England secondary 
school for boys, functioning as a training ground for the American upper‑class 
establishment.[8][12][14] The school is depicted as socially exclusive and as an 
institutional microcosm in which questions of privilege, authority, and moral 
responsibility are played out.[8][10]', 'source_ids': 
['20260623024239665361_f1de7223', '20260623024307745077_3db5b612', 
'20260623024307767432_483bea11', '20260623024239712986_d773ad89'], 'kind': 
'paragraph'}, {'text': 'Some later commentary explicitly describes Justin Martyr 
as a fictional stand‑in for Groton, noting that the book opens with a young 
teacher joining the staff of a private Christian boarding school modeled on the 
school where Auchincloss himself worked, while emphasizing that this is a 
literary reimagining rather than a documentary account.[18][28] At the same time,
essays on New England boarding schools highlight real‑world figures such as 
Endicott Peabody of Groton—known to students as "the Rector" and remembered as a 
stern, patrician headmaster—as archetypes for powerful rectors in fiction, 
situating Frank Prescott in a broader cultural tradition of elite New England 
school leaders.[17][28]', 'source_ids': ['20260623024307812081_a56bb5f6', 
'20260623024643631866_3f1971d6', '20260623024307800562_f98fdda1'], 'kind': 
'paragraph'}]}, {'title': 'Plot summary', 'blocks': [{'text': "The Rector of 
Justin centers on the life of Reverend Francis (Frank) Prescott, D.D., the 
founder and long‑serving rector and headmaster of Justin Martyr, an Episcopal 
boys' school in New England.[12][14][21] As a young man, Prescott attends an 
elite preparatory school, studies divinity at Harvard and Oxford, briefly works 
in New York business, and ends an unsuitable love affair before marrying Harriet;
by about the age of twenty‑five he decides to establish a boys' school modeled on
his own earlier schooling, a decision that shapes the rest of his life.[14]", 
'source_ids': ['20260623024307745077_3db5b612', '20260623024307767432_483bea11', 
'20260623024310821544_600a050b'], 'kind': 'paragraph'}, {'text': "Prescott founds
Justin Martyr and runs it for almost sixty years, governing the institution in a 
harshly religiously doctrinaire and authoritarian manner that increasingly 
clashes with the more secular orientation of the school's board of trustees.[14] 
Over time he becomes out of touch with both the trustees and broader social 
change, even as the school prospers and consolidates its elite 
reputation.[8][10][14]", 'source_ids': ['20260623024307767432_483bea11', 
'20260623024239665361_f1de7223', '20260623024239712986_d773ad89'], 'kind': 
'paragraph'}, {'text': 'The novel opens when Brian Aspinwall, a young and 
inexperienced English literature instructor, arrives at Justin Martyr in the 
early 20th century, feeling lost amid the rituals and hierarchies of the 
school.[14][18] Prescott takes Brian under his wing as a protégé, and Brian 
gradually becomes fascinated with the older man, deciding to investigate 
Prescott’s past and the sources of his authority.[12][14][18]', 'source_ids': 
['20260623024307767432_483bea11', '20260623024307812081_a56bb5f6', 
'20260623024307745077_3db5b612'], 'kind': 'paragraph'}, {'text': 'Brian’s 
investigation provides the frame for the narrative. The story is told primarily 
through Brian’s diaries and through a series of documents and testimonies that he
collects from others, including an unpublished memoir by Prescott’s only 
childhood friend Horace Havistock, statements by Prescott’s youngest daughter, 
accounts from longtime trustee David Griscam, and recollections from Griscam’s 
troubled son Jules.[12][14][18][21] These accounts, interwoven with Brian’s own 
reflections, form an extended inquiry into Prescott’s character and the history 
of the school.', 'source_ids': ['20260623024307745077_3db5b612', 
'20260623024307767432_483bea11', '20260623024307812081_a56bb5f6', 
'20260623024310821544_600a050b'], 'kind': 'paragraph'}, {'text': 'Prescott’s 
family life is depicted as deeply compromised by his devotion to the school. He 
and Harriet have three daughters, whom he admits he does not love enough, and he 
regrets never having had a son; the first two daughters recede into conventional 
domesticity, while the third remains a persistent source of conflict, 
particularly after Prescott intervenes destructively in her love 
affair.[12][14]', 'source_ids': ['20260623024307767432_483bea11', 
'20260623024307745077_3db5b612'], 'kind': 'paragraph'}, {'text': 'Another key 
strand follows Prescott’s long relationship with trustee David Griscam and with 
Griscam’s "soul‑sick" son Jules.[12][14] David, one of Prescott’s early students 
who later becomes a wealthy trustee, is both supporter and critic, while Jules 
experiences a disastrous tenure at the school that culminates in his expulsion in
what one narrator describes as a vindictive act by Prescott.[12][14]', 
'source_ids': ['20260623024307745077_3db5b612', '20260623024307767432_483bea11'],
'kind': 'paragraph'}, {'text': 'As Prescott ages into his eighties, the 
increasingly secular board of trustees, led by David Griscam, pressures him to 
retire; at about eighty‑two he agrees to step down as rector and headmaster.[14] 
He moves to a house just beyond the campus and for a time attempts to undermine 
his successor, but gradually realizes that opinion within the school community 
has turned against him.[14]', 'source_ids': ['20260623024307767432_483bea11'], 
'kind': 'paragraph'}, {'text': 'A few years after his retirement, Prescott 
becomes terminally ill. Initially he faces his prognosis with apparent composure,
but he is later seized by fears about divine judgment and the moral adequacy of 
his life’s work.[14][16] The novel concludes without definitively resolving all 
questions about Prescott’s motives and achievements, leaving Brian and the other 
narrators with an incomplete but multi‑faceted view of the man and his 
school.[14][21]', 'source_ids': ['20260623024307767432_483bea11', 
'20260623024307789750_523a064b', '20260623024310821544_600a050b'], 'kind': 
'paragraph'}]}, {'title': 'Characters', 'blocks': [{'text': 'Francis (Frank) 
Prescott, D.D., is the novel’s central figure: founder, rector, and headmaster of
Justin Martyr, and head minister of its Episcopal community.[12][14][18] He is 
portrayed by different narrators as charismatic, intellectually serious, and 
deeply committed to fusing religious ideals with education, but also as 
authoritarian, emotionally distant, and capable of cruelty and 
vindictiveness.[12][14][16] Contemporary commentary drawn from a New York Times 
obituary describes him as a man of intellect and idealism who can be noble, 
generous and kind, yet also cruel, callous and arbitrary, summarizing the 
tensions that the novel explores.[16]', 'source_ids': 
['20260623024307745077_3db5b612', '20260623024307767432_483bea11', 
'20260623024307812081_a56bb5f6', '20260623024307789750_523a064b'], 'kind': 
'paragraph'}, {'text': 'Brian Aspinwall is a young English teacher at Justin 
Martyr and Prescott’s latest protégé.[12][14][18][21] Described in one review as 
a shy, somewhat effeminate (“epicene”) figure, he keeps diaries and assembles 
memoirs, interviews, and other documents in an attempt to understand 
Prescott.[12][18] Through Brian’s perspective and editorial choices, readers 
receive the fragmented testimonies that make up the narrative.', 'source_ids': 
['20260623024307745077_3db5b612', '20260623024307767432_483bea11', 
'20260623024307812081_a56bb5f6', '20260623024310821544_600a050b'], 'kind': 
'paragraph'}, {'text': 'Horace Havistock, Prescott’s only close childhood friend,
appears in the novel through an unpublished memoir that Brian 
obtains.[12][14][18][21] By the time of the main action, Horace is a "conspicuous
failure," and his recollections of Prescott’s youth provide one of the earliest 
and most personal portraits of the future rector, contrasting early idealism and 
camaraderie with later institutional authority.[12][14][21]', 'source_ids': 
['20260623024307745077_3db5b612', '20260623024307767432_483bea11', 
'20260623024307812081_a56bb5f6', '20260623024310821544_600a050b'], 'kind': 
'paragraph'}, {'text': 'Prescott’s third daughter, whose name is not emphasized 
in critical summaries, contributes another key testimony. She concludes that her 
father has always loved the school more than his family and recounts the story of
a love affair that Prescott effectively destroys, leading some commentators to 
liken her role to that of Cordelia in Shakespeare’s King Lear as a truth‑telling 
yet wounded child.[12][14][21]', 'source_ids': ['20260623024307745077_3db5b612', 
'20260623024307767432_483bea11', '20260623024310821544_600a050b'], 'kind': 
'paragraph'}, {'text': 'David Griscam is introduced as one of Prescott’s first 
students who later becomes a wealthy trustee of Justin Martyr.[12][14] Through 
his recollections and board‑room actions, he emerges as both supporter and 
antagonist: he ultimately leads the effort to secure Prescott’s retirement when 
the rector’s authoritarian style is perceived as out of step with contemporary 
values.[12][14] His son Jules, described as soul‑sick and troubled, has a fraught
relationship with Prescott that culminates in his expulsion from the school, an 
episode some narrators describe as particularly vindictive.[12][14]', 
'source_ids': ['20260623024307745077_3db5b612', '20260623024307767432_483bea11'],
'kind': 'paragraph'}]}, {'title': 'Narrative structure', 'blocks': [{'text': 
'Critics and reviewers consistently note the novel’s distinctive narrative 
structure, in which Brian Aspinwall’s diary entries frame extensive insertions of
other texts and testimonies.[12][14][18] These include Horace Havistock’s memoir,
an interview with Prescott’s daughter, the journals of an earlier young teacher, 
and statements from David and Jules Griscam, all presented largely in their own 
words.[12][14][18]', 'source_ids': ['20260623024307745077_3db5b612', 
'20260623024307767432_483bea11', '20260623024307812081_a56bb5f6'], 'kind': 
'paragraph'}, {'text': 'One detailed analysis describes the book as an "unusual 
novel" that unfolds entirely in the words of Brian or his informants, emphasizing
that the narrative is built from documentary materials rather than conventional 
third‑person narration.[14] The same commentator characterizes it as a mystery 
story in which Brian acts as an amateur detective, puzzling over the 
extraordinary success of an Episcopal boys’ school and the personality of its 
rector, with many questions left unresolved for the reader.[14]', 'source_ids': 
['20260623024307767432_483bea11'], 'kind': 'paragraph'}, {'text': 'The Kirkus 
review terms the book a "prismatic portrait" of Prescott in his eighties, created
from sometimes coincident and sometimes contradictory interpretations by family 
and associates, and asks rhetorically whether he is leviathan, legend, charlatan,
man of God, or even "God among men," underscoring the multiplicity of 
viewpoints.[12] The narrative focus is thus less on external action than on the 
iterative re‑examination of a single life from differing social and psychological
angles.[12][14]', 'source_ids': ['20260623024307745077_3db5b612', 
'20260623024307767432_483bea11'], 'kind': 'paragraph'}]}, {'title': 'Themes and 
analysis', 'blocks': [{'text': 'Reviewers and critics identify The Rector of 
Justin as primarily a character study of Francis Prescott rather than a 
plot‑driven novel.[8][12][14] One critical essay describes it as a character 
study "dressed up like a detective story," with relatively little external action
and an ensemble of narrators who repeatedly return to the mystery of Prescott’s 
personality and influence.[14] The New York Times review similarly emphasizes the
novel’s strength in subtle characterization and detailed social observation 
rather than in dramatic plot twists.[8]', 'source_ids': 
['20260623024239665361_f1de7223', '20260623024307745077_3db5b612', 
'20260623024307767432_483bea11'], 'kind': 'paragraph'}, {'text': 'A major theme 
is the moral and spiritual role of education. In passages quoted by later 
commentators, Prescott asserts that "the only thing a teacher has to go on is 
that rare spark in a boy’s eye" and that teachers should not worry whether a 
student’s enthusiasm is for classical poetry, Icelandic sagas, or laboratory 
experiments, suggesting a broad, pragmatic view of intellectual vocation.[16] He 
also declares that "There is no real distinction between the pulpit and the 
classroom," explaining that he tried to "put God into every book and sport" at 
Justin so as to spread a sense of divine presence throughout school life and make
the school joyful.[16]', 'source_ids': ['20260623024307789750_523a064b'], 'kind':
'paragraph'}, {'text': 'Prescott’s ideal of an integrated religious and 
educational mission coexists with a more hard‑headed, even elitist view of 
private schooling. In another frequently cited passage he remarks that the 
greatness of the private school lies not in producing geniuses, who "grow 
anyway," but in sometimes turning a "third‑rate" student into a "second‑rate" 
one, modest achievements that nonetheless constitute the school’s "glory" even if
they cannot be publicly boasted of.[16] Critics use such statements to show how 
the novel interrogates both the aspirations and the limitations of elite 
education.[8][16]', 'source_ids': ['20260623024307789750_523a064b', 
'20260623024239665361_f1de7223'], 'kind': 'paragraph'}, {'text': 'The book also 
explores the personal costs of institutional devotion. Through testimonies from 
Prescott’s daughter and others, the novel portrays a man who appears to love his 
school more than his family, with his three daughters feeling neglected or 
sacrificed to his work and one daughter’s romantic life being thwarted by his 
interventions.[12][14][21] This tension contributes to the portrait of Prescott 
as simultaneously admirable and morally compromised, a dynamic that later critics
link to broader questions about leadership and responsibility in elite 
institutions.[9][16]', 'source_ids': ['20260623024307745077_3db5b612', 
'20260623024307767432_483bea11', '20260623024310821544_600a050b', 
'20260623024239693087_6e1a88ad', '20260623024307789750_523a064b'], 'kind': 
'paragraph'}, {'text': 'Several commentators read the school itself as a 
metaphorical microcosm of the American upper‑class establishment. The original 
New York Times review describes Justin Martyr as a crucible in which questions of
authority, privilege, and moral compromise are dramatized rather than as a simple
vehicle for attacking the upper class.[8] Later essays on Auchincloss’s work 
similarly point to The Rector of Justin as a high point in his examination of 
upper‑class institutions and the ethical responsibilities of those who lead 
them.[9][39]', 'source_ids': ['20260623024239665361_f1de7223', 
'20260623024239693087_6e1a88ad', '20260623025358968000_a5c92a21'], 'kind': 
'paragraph'}]}, {'title': 'Reception', 'blocks': [{'text': 'Upon publication in 
1964, The Rector of Justin received substantial attention from major review 
outlets. In The New York Times, Granville Hicks treated the novel as a serious 
and ambitious work about an elite boys’ boarding school and its aging headmaster,
praising Auchincloss’s command of multiple narrators and the psychological depth 
of his portrait of Prescott.[8] Hicks emphasized the subtlety of the 
characterization and the detailed depiction of the school’s social milieu, noting
that the book’s strengths lay more in close observation than in plot 
surprises.[8]', 'source_ids': ['20260623024239665361_f1de7223'], 'kind': 
'paragraph'}, {'text': 'Kirkus Reviews described the book as a "prismatic 
portrait" of Reverend Francis Prescott at the end of "eighty forceful years," 
assembled from sometimes coincident and sometimes contradictory interpretations 
by his family and associates, with Brian Aspinwall acting as the compiler of 
evidence.[12] The review highlighted the range of perspectives—from a decadent 
school friend’s memoir to the confidences of Prescott’s third daughter and the 
testimony of a former student turned trustee and his expelled son—and 
characterized the novel as one of Auchincloss’s more substantial works, going 
"beyond and below" the superficial sheen of his earlier society settings.[12]', 
'source_ids': ['20260623024307745077_3db5b612'], 'kind': 'paragraph'}, {'text': 
'In year‑end coverage of 1964 fiction, a New York Times article titled "A Sorting
Out" referred to The Rector of Justin as both a best seller and a serious 
contender for the year’s major fiction awards, contrasting its accomplished 
traditional realism with the more formally innovative style of Saul Bellow’s 
Herzog.[10] The article treated Auchincloss’s novel as a respected but 
stylistically conventional entrant in the contemporary literary field, implicitly
acknowledging its high quality while suggesting that it was less experimental 
than some award‑winning peers.[10]', 'source_ids': 
['20260623024239712986_d773ad89'], 'kind': 'paragraph'}, {'text': 'Subsequent 
commentary has continued to regard the novel highly within Auchincloss’s oeuvre. 
A 1979 New York Times essay, "In Praise of Louis Auchincloss," singled out The 
Rector of Justin as one of his major or defining novels, noting its popular and 
critical success and treating it as a high point in his exploration of 
upper‑class institutions and moral responsibility.[9] Biographical and critical 
studies of Auchincloss similarly cite the novel as among his most accomplished 
works.[20][37][39]', 'source_ids': ['20260623024239693087_6e1a88ad', 
'20260623025358873483_9e482c87', '20260623025358946389_0fadf170', 
'20260623025358968000_a5c92a21'], 'kind': 'paragraph'}, {'text': 'Later reviewers
have also praised the novel’s enduring relevance. A 2008 Washington Post essay 
discussing lessons about teaching and education reform from The Rector of Justin 
highlighted its insights into the ideals and limitations of private schooling, 
while a 2020 Education Next article argued that the book "holds up pretty well" 
decades after publication and remains useful for thinking about contemporary 
debates on education and politics.[15][16]', 'source_ids': 
['20260623024307777748_20df517f', '20260623024307789750_523a064b'], 'kind': 
'paragraph'}]}, {'title': 'Awards and distinctions', 'blocks': [{'text': 'The 
Rector of Justin was a finalist for the 1965 National Book Award for Fiction, as 
recorded in the National Book Foundation’s official listing of award titles, 
where it appears with the status designation "Finalist" for that year’s fiction 
category.[27] A New York Times obituary quoted in a later Education Next article 
notes that the novel was a bestseller and a National Book Award finalist and 
states that it is regarded by many critics as Auchincloss’s best and most 
important novel.[16]', 'source_ids': ['20260623024643574785_d622c032', 
'20260623024307789750_523a064b'], 'kind': 'paragraph'}]}, {'title': 'Editions', 
'blocks': [{'text': 'Bibliographic records and bookseller listings indicate that 
The Rector of Justin has remained in print in various formats since its original 
release. A 1980 Houghton Mifflin edition is cataloged in WorldCat as a Boston 
publication with original copyright date 1964, suggesting a reissue for new 
readers.[29]', 'source_ids': ['20260623024643651710_5aaa1047'], 'kind': 
'paragraph'}, {'text': 'A widely distributed trade paperback edition has been 
issued by Mariner Books, an imprint associated with Houghton Mifflin, carrying 
ISBN 0618224890; publisher and vendor descriptions for this edition repeat 
promotional copy describing the novel as one of Auchincloss’s most accomplished 
works centering on Frank Prescott, founder of an exclusive boys’ 
school.[30][31]', 'source_ids': ['20260623024643664744_593c9c25', 
'20260623024643677485_51f1bfc6'], 'kind': 'paragraph'}, {'text': 'Online catalogs
and sales listings for 1964 hardcovers marketed as first editions or first 
printings, often in dust jackets, indicate continued interest among collectors in
early Houghton Mifflin printings of the novel.[4][5][6]', 'source_ids': 
['20260623024132099428_a42b4739', '20260623024132110240_515cff7d', 
'20260623024132121106_8cfcc34b'], 'kind': 'paragraph'}]}, {'title': 'Author 
context and legacy', 'blocks': [{'text': 'The Rector of Justin occupies a 
prominent place in Louis Auchincloss’s body of work. Biographical and critical 
accounts routinely list it among his most important novels, and publisher 
descriptions and reference entries describe it as one of his most accomplished, 
sometimes explicitly calling it his masterpiece.[7][20][21][30][31] The book’s 
focus on an elite educational institution aligns with Auchincloss’s broader 
interest in American upper‑class environments and professional elites, subjects 
he also explored in novels about Wall Street lawyers, old New York families, and 
other bastions of establishment power.[9][20][37][39]', 'source_ids': 
['20260623024132132466_776229e4', '20260623025358909950_3127f4f', 
'20260623024310821544_600a050b', '20260623024643664744_593c9c25', 
'20260623024643677485_51f1bfc6', '20260623024239693087_6e1a88ad', 
'20260623025358946389_0fadf170', '20260623025358968000_a5c92a21'], 'kind': 
'paragraph'}, {'text': 'Obituaries and institutional tributes note that 
Auchincloss’s own background—in particular his education at Groton and his 
professional life in New York law—supplied material for his portraits of elite 
institutions, a connection often invoked when discussing The Rector of Justin’s 
depiction of a New England boarding school.[28][38] Critics have pointed to the 
novel’s combination of psychological insight and detailed social observation as a
culmination of his realist method, contributing to its reputation as a defining 
work in his career.[8][9][20][39]', 'source_ids': 
['20260623024643631866_3f1971d6', '20260623025358956356_017d8e81', 
'20260623024239665361_f1de7223', '20260623024239693087_6e1a88ad', 
'20260623025358909950_3127f4f', '20260623025358968000_a5c92a21'], 'kind': 
'paragraph'}, {'text': 'Later discussions of education have drawn on the novel 
for its depiction of the ideals and contradictions of private schooling. A 
Washington Post essay and an Education Next article both use The Rector of Justin
to reflect on questions of teaching, the mission of private schools, and the 
integration of religious ideals into school life, suggesting that the book 
continues to be read not only as a literary work but also as a commentary on 
educational leadership and reform.[15][16]', 'source_ids': 
['20260623024307777748_20df517f', '20260623024307789750_523a064b'], 'kind': 
'paragraph'}]}, {'title': 'Conflicting interpretations and debates', 'blocks': 
[{'text': 'Some secondary sources differ in the emphasis they place on the 
relationship between Justin Martyr and real New England boarding schools. A 
detailed blog analysis describes the fictional school as closely resembling the 
author’s own preparatory‑school experience, while another essay on New England 
headmasters highlights that readers and alumni of real institutions such as 
Groton have sometimes interpreted the novel as a thinly veiled portrait of their 
school, indicating varying degrees of perceived one‑to‑one correspondence between
fiction and reality.[14][17]', 'source_ids': ['20260623024307767432_483bea11', 
'20260623024307800562_f98fdda1'], 'kind': 'paragraph'}, {'text': 'There is also 
variation in how sources describe the novel’s awards history and stature. A New 
York Times obituary passage quoted in Education Next describes The Rector of 
Justin as a bestseller and a finalist for the National Book Award and reports 
that many critics regard it as Auchincloss’s best and most important novel, while
the National Book Foundation’s official listing records the book’s status 
specifically as a 1965 National Book Awards finalist for fiction, without ranking
it among the winners; together these accounts confirm its finalist status while 
differing in the rhetorical weight they give to the nomination and to claims of 
pre‑eminence within Auchincloss’s oeuvre.[16][27]', 'source_ids': 
['20260623024307789750_523a064b', '20260623024643574785_d622c032'], 'kind': 
